# The runtime thesis — everything is emulated in WASM

The load-bearing invariant of the Workbooks runtime: **untrusted code NEVER runs natively.** It is
compiled by a WASM-native compiler to WebAssembly and runs in a WASM sandbox — Wasmtime today, with
**TinyLasers** (a pure-BEAM WASM engine) taking over. Everything the guest expects — bash, the
filesystem, processes, POSIX — is **emulated** to whatever degree the guest needs. The guest only has to
BELIEVE it's real.

**When to use:** before planning anything that assumes a real shell, filesystem, or process — those
assumptions are wrong here and will burn turns.

## The mappings (what the guest believes → what actually happens)
- **bash** → `washy` (the `sh.c` shell compiled to WASM, run on the tiny-lasers WASM→BEAM substrate).
- **pipes** → in-memory buffering (no fork).
- **fork/exec** → function calls for builtins; `host_exec` for untrusted programs.
- **filesystem** → a virtual FS (`Nexus.Store` + SQLite, one DB per workspace; `/work` is a view).
- **POSIX** → a WASI slice only; the process model is host-side emulation.

## What this rules out (a whole class of wrong plans)
- "Just run bash / a native binary" — there is no native execution for untrusted code.
- "Write to the real filesystem" — writes go through the virtual FS (the Store).
- "Shell out to a process" — there is no process model; use the nexus's own effects.
- The one **escape hatch** (LOCAL, trusted agent only): a `Nexus.Forge` micro-VM for a build the WASM
  sandbox genuinely can't host — a failsafe, never a default (`mix forge run "<cmd>"`).

## Why
Isolation + density + byte-exactness, all on the BEAM. A guest can't exhaust the host, can't name a host
module, can't escape — because the escape is *unexpressible*, not merely blocked. This is why "we need
real bash / a real filesystem / wasmer for the real thing" is always wrong: the emulation IS the product.
